@Edward Rice You shouldn't conflate "reduce by N%" with "reduce by a factor of N". They are separate phrases with very different meanings.

"Factor" implies multiplication; therefore "Reduce/Increase by a factor of N", expanded, means "make closer to/farther from zero, using a multiplication, involving N". It'll be obvious from context whether you multiply by N or 1/N.

No trained communicator would write "reduce by a factor of 1" except as a joke.

